stream
BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (The constructive part: learning is what the student does) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Constructivism claims that understanding is built by the learner. New information) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (becomes knowledge only when the student connects it with what he or she already knows,) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (tests it and uses it.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (This has a practical consequence that is easy to state and hard to accept: what the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (student does is more important for learning than what the teacher does. A brilliant) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (lecture produces little learning if students only listen and copy. A modest task) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (produces a lot of learning if it makes students explain, compare, apply and argue.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Biggs illustrates this with two imaginary students. Susan is academically committed: she) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (comes prepared, asks herself questions and reflects on the material almost without help.) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Robert is at university to get a qualification: in a traditional lecture he takes notes) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (and memorises them.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Good teaching is not measured by Susan, who learns in any case. It is measured by) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (Robert: does the design of the course make him use the higher-level thinking that Susan) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (uses spontaneously? Constructive alignment is a way to achieve exactly this.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (2) Tj ET
endstream
endobj
