BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (Teaching and learning activities) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Once the outcomes are written, the teacher chooses activities that make students perform) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (the verbs.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (If the outcome is "explain", suitable activities include peer teaching, short written) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (explanations, and answering "why" questions in a tutorial. If the outcome is "apply",) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (students need case studies, problem-based learning, laboratory or project work. If the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (outcome is "reflect", they need a reflective journal or a portfolio with feedback.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (A lecture is not forbidden, but it mainly activates listening and note-taking. It is) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (aligned with outcomes such as "describe" and it is poorly aligned with outcomes such as) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf ("design" or "evaluate". A lecture can be improved by inserting short tasks: a question) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (to discuss with a neighbour, a one-minute paper, a prediction before a demonstration.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (The principle is simple: students learn what they practise. Time on task should be) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (distributed according to the outcomes. If the most important outcome receives the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (smallest share of student activity, the course is not aligned, whatever the syllabus) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (claims.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (5) Tj ET
endstream
