BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (Deep and surface approaches to learning) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Research on student learning distinguishes two approaches to a task.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (In a surface approach the student intends to get the task out of the way with minimum) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (effort. Typical activities are memorising without understanding, listing points instead) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (of building an argument, and reproducing the words of the source.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (In a deep approach the student intends to understand. He or she relates new ideas to) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (previous knowledge, looks for principles, checks evidence and tries to explain the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (material in personal words.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (An approach is not a fixed trait of a person. The same student uses a deep approach in) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (one course and a surface approach in another, depending on the teaching context. Factors) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (that push towards surface learning include excessive workload, assessment that rewards) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (recall, lack of feedback and anxiety. Factors that support deep learning include clear) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (outcomes, active tasks, assessment that requires understanding, and the opportunity to) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (make mistakes safely.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Constructive alignment is a way of building a context in which the deep approach is the) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (easiest way to succeed.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (8) Tj ET
endstream
