BT 14 TL 56 780 Td
/F2 15 Tf (A worked example: redesigning a programming course) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Original design. Outcome: "Students will understand algorithm complexity". Teaching:) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (lectures with slides. Assessment: a written test with definitions of O-notation and) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (questions such as "What is the complexity of quicksort?") Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Problems. The verb "understand" cannot be observed. Students practise listening. The) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (test rewards memorised answers.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Redesigned outcome: "Students will be able to analyse the time complexity of an) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (unfamiliar algorithm and justify the choice between two algorithms for a given task.") Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Redesigned activities: in each seminar pairs of students receive code they have not) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (seen, estimate its complexity, and explain their reasoning to another pair. The teacher) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (asks probing questions: why, what if the input is already sorted, where is the mistake) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (in this analysis.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Redesigned assessment: a short oral defence. The student receives a new piece of code,) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (analyses it aloud and answers follow-up questions. The rubric describes levels of) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (quality, from "names the notation" to "generalises to a new situation".) Tj T*
T*
/F1 11 Tf (Now the verb "analyse and justify" is present in all three components, and memorising) Tj T*
/F1 11 Tf (slides no longer helps.) Tj T*
ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 290 40 Td (9) Tj ET
endstream
